meta {
  name: Download Quote Document
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/quote-recaps/{{createdQuoteRecapId}}/document/download
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns a presigned URL plus the display metadata", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("downloadUrl");
    expect(body).to.have.property("filename");
    expect(body).to.have.property("contentType");
    expect(body).to.have.property("expiresIn");
    expect(body.filename).to.equal("carrier-quote.pdf");
    expect(body.contentType).to.equal("application/pdf");
    expect(body.expiresIn).to.be.a("number");
  });

  test("signs the URL for inline display, not download", function () {
    // The whole point of the endpoint: an attachment disposition would hand the
    // user a file in their downloads folder instead of a rendered PDF.
    const url = decodeURIComponent(res.getBody().downloadUrl);
    expect(url).to.contain("response-content-disposition=inline");
  });

  test("never leaks the storage key", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("key");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /quote-recaps/:id/document/download — open the quote document (PAC-56 #10, #30)

  Returns a **short-lived presigned GET** for the document uploaded with the
  recap. The client opens it in a new tab, where the browser's own PDF viewer
  renders it; the user downloads from there.

  Replaces legacy's SmartSuite native file viewer, which was lost in the move
  off SmartSuite. David asked for view/download on the quote document (#10);
  #30 generalises it to every file on the Lead Detail page.

  - **Module:** `quote_recaps` (must be enabled for the agency)
  - **Permission:** `quote_recaps:read` — viewing a document whose metadata you
    can already see is a read, not a write
  - **Data scope:** clamped to the caller's scope on the **recap**, 404 (never
    403) for another producer's — `LeadAccessService.assertOwned`

  ## Path params
  | param | type     | notes                        |
  |-------|----------|------------------------------|
  | id    | ObjectId | the quote recap, not the lead |

  ## Why inline, and why signed per request
  The URL carries `response-content-disposition=inline` and an explicit
  `response-content-type`, both fixed at signing time so whoever holds the link
  cannot change them. The content type is overridden rather than trusted from
  storage: an object stored as `application/octet-stream` downloads instead of
  rendering however the disposition is set.

  The client fetches this **per click** rather than baking a URL into an
  `href`. The URL expires (`STORAGE_DOWNLOAD_URL_TTL_SECONDS`, default 300s), so
  one rendered at page load would be dead by the time a producer clicked it —
  and would put a live document URL into the page markup.

  The storage key is never returned. It is an internal path, and a client that
  knows where the bytes live is a client that can be pointed at someone else's.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "downloadUrl": "http://localhost:9100/sfa-uploads/agencies/.../carrier-quote.pdf?X-Amz-Signature=...",
    "filename": "carrier-quote.pdf",
    "contentType": "application/pdf",
    "expiresIn": 300
  }
  ```

  ## Errors
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `quote_recaps` module disabled, or missing `quote_recaps:read`.
  - `404 Not Found` — unknown/malformed id, a recap outside the caller's data
    scope, **or** a recap that carries no document. All three are deliberately
    the same answer.

  ## Run-order note
  Depends on `createdQuoteRecapId` from `Create Quote Recap` (seq 4), which
  attaches `carrier-quote.pdf`. Running this request on its own will 404.
}
